      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="overdrawRangeMinDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Layer count that maps to solid blue.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>1</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>1000000.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>2.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="overdrawRangeMaxDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Layer count that maps to solid red.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>1</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>1000000.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>100.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="10" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="maxLayerDensityLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Max layer density</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Caps LoD traversal: a node estimated to be this dense with overdraw (see the "Show overdraw" ramp above - the same layer-count scale) is kept as its own merged approximation rather than refined further, regardless of pixel scale limit. Use the overdraw view to find a problem area's layer count, then set this a bit below it. 0 = disabled (no effect on the visualisation above - only on real rendering). Applies live, on the next camera movement.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="10" column="1">
    <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="maxLayerDensityDoubleSpinBox">
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Caps LoD traversal: a node estimated to be this dense with overdraw (see the "Show overdraw" ramp above - the same layer-count scale) is kept as its own merged approximation rather than refined further, regardless of pixel scale limit. Use the overdraw view to find a problem area's layer count, then set this a bit below it. 0 = disabled (no effect on the visualisation above - only on real rendering). Applies live, on the next camera movement.</string>
     </property>
     <property name="decimals">
      <number>1</number>
     </property>
     <property name="minimum">
      <double>0.000000000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="maximum">
      <double>1000000.000000000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="singleStep">
      <double>1.000000000000000</double>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="11" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="maxTreeDepthLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Max tree depth</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Hard global ceiling on how many levels traversal may unfold from the tree root, everywhere in the world - independent of pixel scale limit / max layer density / max splats budget. A node at this depth is always kept as its own merged approximation, never refined further. 0 = disabled (no ceiling). Applies live, on the next camera movement.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="11" column="1">
    <widget class="QSpinBox" name="maxTreeDepthSpinBox">
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Hard global ceiling on how many levels traversal may unfold from the tree root, everywhere in the world - independent of pixel scale limit / max layer density / max splats budget. A node at this depth is always kept as its own merged approximation, never refined further. 0 = disabled (no ceiling). Applies live, on the next camera movement.</string>
     </property>
     <property name="minimum">
      <number>0</number>
     </property>
     <property name="maximum">
      <number>1000</number>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="12" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="numDrawSlicesLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Draw slices</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>How many consecutive sub-ranges each cloud's depth-sorted splats are drawn in, nearest first. 1 = one draw per cloud. On its own this must not change the image at all: the ranges are drawn back to back in the same order, so any value should look identical. It exists so a front-to-back saturation test has somewhere to run between draws. Applies live, immediately.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="12" column="1">
    <widget class="QSpinBox" name="numDrawSlicesSpinBox">
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>How many consecutive sub-ranges each cloud's depth-sorted splats are drawn in, nearest first. 1 = one draw per cloud. On its own this must not change the image at all: the ranges are drawn back to back in the same order, so any value should look identical. It exists so a front-to-back saturation test has somewhere to run between draws. Applies live, immediately.</string>
     </property>
     <property name="minimum">
      <number>1</number>
     </property>
     <property name="maximum">
      <number>1024</number>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="13" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="sliceGrowthLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Slice growth</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>How much bigger each draw slice is than the one before it. 1 = all slices the same size; below 1 = a coarse first slice and a finely sliced tail. Decides where the saturation test gets to look. Nothing is saturated over roughly the first fifth of a depth-sorted cloud (it takes about 63 layers at the measured mean alpha), so a boundary inside that region costs a full-screen pass and skips nothing, while every check past it catches a new batch of finished pixels. Changes nothing on its own, like the slice count itself. Applies live, immediately.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="13" column="1">
    <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="sliceGrowthDoubleSpinBox">
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>How much bigger each draw slice is than the one before it. 1 = all slices the same size; below 1 = a coarse first slice and a finely sliced tail. Decides where the saturation test gets to look. Nothing is saturated over roughly the first fifth of a depth-sorted cloud (it takes about 63 layers at the measured mean alpha), so a boundary inside that region costs a full-screen pass and skips nothing, while every check past it catches a new batch of finished pixels. Changes nothing on its own, like the slice count itself. Applies live, immediately.</string>
     </property>
     <property name="decimals">
      <number>2</number>
     </property>
     <property name="minimum">
      <double>0.250000000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="maximum">
      <double>16.000000000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="singleStep">
      <double>0.050000000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="value">
      <double>1.000000000000000</double>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="14" column="0">
    <widget class="QCheckBox" name="saturationGateCheckBox">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Saturation gate</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Skips splats at pixels the front-to-back composite has already finished with: between draw slices, pixels whose accumulated coverage has reached the threshold are marked, and later slices are rejected there before being shaded. Needs more than one draw slice to do anything. Off in the overdraw views, where the accumulated alpha counts layers rather than coverage.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="14" column="1">
    <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="saturationThresholdDoubleSpinBox">
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Accumulated coverage at or above which a pixel counts as finished. 0.9961 (= 1 - 1/255) means the light still getting through is under one 8-bit level, which is where the reference 3DGS rasteriser stops. Lowering it skips more work and eventually shows. The accumulation buffer is 16-bit float, whose steps near 1.0 are about 0.0005 wide, so values closer to 1 than that make no difference.</string>
     </property>
     <property name="decimals">
      <number>4</number>
     </property>
     <property name="minimum">
      <double>0.000000000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="maximum">
      <double>1.000000000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="singleStep">
      <double>0.005000000000000</double>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="15" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="saturationMaskDownscaleLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Mask downscale</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>How many screen pixels across one texel of the saturation gate's mask covers. 1 = a full-resolution mask, 4 = a mask with a sixteenth of the pixels. A texel is marked only when every pixel under it is finished, so coarsening the mask never skips a pixel that was still being drawn - the only loss is the partly finished texels around the edge of a saturated region. Making the mask small is what makes checking often affordable. Rebuilds the mask when changed.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="15" column="1">
    <widget class="QSpinBox" name="saturationMaskDownscaleSpinBox">
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>How many screen pixels across one texel of the saturation gate's mask covers. 1 = a full-resolution mask, 4 = a mask with a sixteenth of the pixels. A texel is marked only when every pixel under it is finished, so coarsening the mask never skips a pixel that was still being drawn - the only loss is the partly finished texels around the edge of a saturated region. Making the mask small is what makes checking often affordable. Rebuilds the mask when changed.</string>
     </property>
     <property name="minimum">
      <number>1</number>
     </property>
     <property name="maximum">
      <number>16</number>
     </property>
     <property name="value">
      <number>4</number>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="16" column="0" colspan="2">
    <widget class="QCheckBox" name="accumBuffer8BitCheckBox">
     <property name="text">
      <string>8-bit accumulation buffer</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Blends the splats into an RGBA8 buffer instead of the default RGBA16F, halving the bytes each fragment writes. The splat pass is mostly bound by blend bandwidth, so this is worth about a third of it. The cost is precision: hundreds of blends accumulate per pixel and the resolve then divides the accumulated colour by the accumulated coverage, so quantisation is amplified where coverage is low - cloud silhouettes and thin sparse regions, not the dense areas. It also weakens the saturation gate, whose saving is the blending it avoids. Rebuilds the accumulation framebuffer when toggled.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="17" column="0">
    <widget class="QPushButton" name="frustumReportPushButton">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Frustum report</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>One-off structure report (not live), written to the log: for every cloud in view, how much of the LoD tree is unfolded at this camera position, and for each drawn node why the traversal stopped there - converged, capped by density/depth/budget, or a leaf with nothing coarser above it. Also the trees' depth and branching, and the layer_density of what is being drawn. Runs a full traversal per cloud on the main thread, so expect a hitch on a large cloud.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="17" column="1">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="frustumReportResultLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string></string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="18" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="distClampLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Distance slice min/max (m)</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Slices the cloud open by distance from the camera, so you can look inside it a shell at a time. Shows only splats whose distance from the camera lies inside [min, max] metres; tick Invert to hide that shell instead and see through it. The distance is measured from the camera position, so the slice is a spherical shell and turning on the spot does not change what survives. Default (0, 1000) shows everything. Applies live, in the vertex shader - no re-traversal, and free when left wide open.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="18" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="distClampWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="distClampHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="distClampMinDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>2</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>10000.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.250000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="distClampMaxDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>2</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>10000.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.250000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>1000.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="distClampInvertCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string>Invert</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Unchecked (default): show only what is inside [min, max]. Checked: hide that shell and show everything outside it - which is how you cut away the near wall and look into the room behind it.</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="19" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="mergeTolLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Merge tol: colour / angle</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Only affects the Frustum report's merge section - how alike two splats have to be before the report will treat them as the same surface seen twice. Colour is the allowed difference per channel (0-1); angle is the allowed difference in facing, in degrees. Defaults 0.10 and 26. Run the report once at the defaults and once at a deliberately reckless setting (say 0.4 and 60) - the gap between the two answers says how much of the merge prize the safety conditions are costing.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="19" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="mergeTolWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="mergeTolHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
